WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.104 --> 00:00:08.442 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Asia episode three of seven. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.103 --> 00:00:24.242 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We circle Arabia through seven countries: Saudi Arabia, Yemen, Oman, the
United Arab Emirates, Qatar, Bahrain and Kuwait. Bahrain uses a labelled
supplemental map because GeoClick omits it.

3
00:00:24.954 --> 00:00:28.750 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Saudi Arabia and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:38.223 --> 00:00:50.971 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Saudi Arabia fills most of the Arabian Peninsula, between the Red Sea and the
Gulf. Jordan and Iraq are north, Yemen south, and Oman toward the southeast.

5
00:00:51.634 --> 00:01:02.722 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Hegra preserves monumental rock-cut tombs made by the Nabataeans, the same
civilisation associated with Petra. Desert routes linked these places across
modern borders.

6
00:01:03.374 --> 00:01:13.695 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the peninsula’s large central country, with Hegra’s carved tombs as
the hook. The smaller Gulf countries cluster along its eastern side.

7
00:01:14.491 --> 00:01:17.637 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Yemen and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:28.063 --> 00:01:37.107 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yemen occupies the southwestern corner of Arabia, below Saudi Arabia and west
of Oman. Its shores face the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden.

9
00:01:37.783 --> 00:01:49.730 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Old Sana’a is famous for tall traditional tower houses in a mountain valley.
Several-storey earthen buildings make an unexpected skyline in this part of
Arabia.

10
00:01:50.392 --> 00:02:00.713 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture tower houses above the southwest coast. Yemen is the peninsula’s
lower-left country; Oman is the lower-right neighbour farther east.

11
00:02:03.355 --> 00:02:15.000 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Oman curves around Arabia’s southeastern edge, east of Yemen and south of the
United Arab Emirates. Its separate Musandam territory overlooks the Strait of
Hormuz.

12
00:02:15.655 --> 00:02:28.275 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Oman’s aflaj irrigation systems move spring or underground water by gravity.
Communities share that water for farms and households, making careful
cooperation part of the engineering.

13
00:02:28.928 --> 00:02:42.314 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the southeastern curve and its gravity-fed channels. The small
northern Musandam piece also belongs to Oman, even though the Emirates
separate it from the main territory.

14
00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:47.760 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring United Arab Emirates and its neighbours into view.

15
00:02:54.312 --> 00:03:06.412 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The United Arab Emirates lies along the southern Gulf coast, between Saudi
Arabia and Oman. Dubai and Abu Dhabi are cities within this country, not
separate countries.

16
00:03:07.072 --> 00:03:21.399 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The oases around Al Ain preserve traces of settled life in the desert,
including ancient tombs, wells and irrigation works. The country’s history
reaches far beyond modern skyscrapers.

17
00:03:22.064 --> 00:03:34.777 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture the coastal federation beside Oman, with inland oases behind its
cities. Keep the country’s long name together: United Arab Emirates is one
stop on our map.

18
00:03:35.513 --> 00:03:38.833 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Qatar and its neighbours into view.

19
00:03:43.241 --> 00:03:53.818 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Qatar is the small peninsula projecting north from Arabia into the Gulf. Saudi
Arabia is its only land neighbour; Bahrain lies offshore to the northwest.

20
00:03:54.473 --> 00:04:03.598 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Al Zubarah was a trading and pearling town. Wind-blown sand protected parts of
its streets, houses and harbour remains after the settlement declined.

21
00:04:04.258 --> 00:04:16.843 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Qatar as the little thumb of land pointing into the Gulf. Picture a
trading town preserved beneath sand, and keep the peninsula separate from
island Bahrain.

22
00:04:18.081 --> 00:04:23.921 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's find Bahrain on a labelled supplemental map, then move in for a closer
view.

23
00:04:39.525 --> 00:04:52.273 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bahrain is an island country in the Gulf, east of Saudi Arabia and northwest
of Qatar. This labelled supplemental map adds it because GeoClick leaves it
out.

24
00:04:52.936 --> 00:05:05.684 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bahrain’s pearling heritage connects oyster beds offshore with merchants’
houses and storehouses in Muharraq. The sea supported an economy long before
oil became central to the region.

25
00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:18.832 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture pearls beside an island: Bahrain. Qatar is a peninsula attached to
Arabia; Bahrain is offshore. Keep their two small shapes distinct.

26
00:05:19.825 --> 00:05:23.494 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kuwait and its neighbours into view.

27
00:05:27.908 --> 00:05:39.890 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kuwait sits at the northwestern head of the Gulf, below Iraq and above Saudi
Arabia. It is a small coastal country, far north of Qatar and the Emirates.

28
00:05:40.552 --> 00:05:55.227 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Al Sadu is a traditional Bedouin weaving craft preserved in Kuwait. Geometric
textiles were used for tents and other practical objects, carrying desert
knowledge into woven patterns.

29
00:05:55.877 --> 00:06:05.641 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Think woven tent fabric at the top of the Gulf. Place Kuwait below Iraq first,
then look south along the coast for Bahrain, Qatar and the Emirates.

30
00:06:11.021 --> 00:06:26.845 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Saudi Arabia fills the centre. Yemen is southwest, Oman southeast, the
Emirates along the southern Gulf, Qatar the small peninsula, Bahrain the
nearby islands, and Kuwait at the Gulf’s northern head.

31
00:06:27.503 --> 00:06:42.259 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember rock-cut tombs, tower houses, irrigation channels, oases, a
sand-covered trading town, pearls and weaving. Keep peninsula Qatar separate
from island Bahrain.
